From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Sun, 12 Oct 2026 04:10:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Collect sections without losing what an RT-11 module needs

Collecting sections kept neither what an RT-11 object module refers to
nor the file that says where its lines are.  Both are needed to build a
program whose halves are in different languages -- which these targets
can, ld reading an RT-11 object module as readily as an ELF one (bfd's
obj-pdp11) -- and neither failure said anything.

The first: --gc-sections works out what is reachable by following
relocations, and it follows them only through ELF objects, the marking
being done with ELF internal relocations.  An RT-11 object module's
relocations are not those, so what such a file refers to was invisible.
A C function called only from the MACRO-11 half of a program had its
section collected, and the relocation in the module was then against a
symbol with no section -- which is given zero, so the JSR went to
whatever followed it.  The program hung, and nothing in the link had
said a word.  -Wl,-u,NAME answered it one name at a time, for anyone who
knew to.

What a file refers to is what it leaves undefined, and that much is
plain to read whatever the format.  So bfd_elf_gc_sections now keeps
every symbol a non-ELF input leaves undefined, exactly as --undefined
keeps one, right where it already keeps the symbols of --undefined
itself (bed->gc_keep).

The second: an input file's debug sections are kept only when some
allocatable section of that same file is kept, which is right for an
object whose code was collected -- its line numbers describe nothing
now -- and wrong for a file that never had code in it.  macro11's -elf
writes exactly such a file: the line numbers and symbols of a MACRO-11
module as DWARF, to be linked beside the object module that holds the
code (see patches/macro11/0002).  It has no allocatable section to keep,
so every --gc-sections link threw all of it away and the MACRO-11 half
of a program lost its line numbers.

So the test is now whether the file had an allocatable section at all:
when it had none, nothing of it has been discarded and its debug
information describes code that is still there.

Checked: examples/macro11mix (C calling MACRO-11 and MACRO-11 calling
back into C) links with --gc-sections and -ffunction-sections, runs on
the machine, and addr2line maps the addresses of both halves to their
own source lines -- where before the C function the assembler called was
gone and the MACRO-11 line table with it.  Collecting still collects:
that program's image is 10752 bytes with --gc-sections and 13824
without, and the debug sections of library members nothing reaches are
dropped as they were (55 sections in that link).  And nothing changes
for a program that is all ELF: examples/hello and examples/sin come out
byte for byte identical, .sav and .elf alike, to a build with the
linker as it was.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 bfd/elflink.c | 94 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 92 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff -urN a/bfd/elflink.c b/bfd/elflink.c
--- a/bfd/elflink.c	2026-10-12 04:10:00
+++ b/bfd/elflink.c	2026-10-12 04:10:00
@@ -14257,6 +14257,7 @@
     {
       asection *isec;
       bool some_kept;
+      bool some_alloc;
       bool debug_frag_seen;
       bool has_kept_debug_info;
 
@@ -14270,8 +14271,13 @@
 	 see if any other section is already marked,
 	 and note if we have any fragmented debug sections.  */
       debug_frag_seen = some_kept = has_kept_debug_info = false;
+      some_alloc = false;
       for (isec = ibfd->sections; isec != NULL; isec = isec->next)
 	{
+	  if ((isec->flags & (SEC_ALLOC | SEC_LINKER_CREATED)) == SEC_ALLOC
+	      && elf_section_type (isec) != SHT_NOTE)
+	    some_alloc = true;
+
 	  if ((isec->flags & SEC_LINKER_CREATED) != 0)
 	    isec->gc_mark = 1;
 	  else if (isec->gc_mark
@@ -14316,8 +14322,16 @@
 	}
 
       /* If no non-note alloc section in this file will be kept, then
-	 we can toss out the debug and special sections.  */
-      if (!some_kept)
+	 we can toss out the debug and special sections -- unless the
+	 file had no such section to begin with, in which case nothing
+	 of it has been discarded and its debug information describes
+	 code that is still there.  That is a file written to carry
+	 debug information and nothing else, which is how the line
+	 numbers of a module in another format can reach an ELF link:
+	 for the pdp11-rt11 targets, macro11's -elf writes the line
+	 numbers and symbols of a MACRO-11 module as DWARF beside the
+	 RT-11 object module that holds its code.  */
+      if (!some_kept && some_alloc)
 	continue;
 
       /* Keep debug and special sections like .comment when they are
@@ -14644,7 +14658,77 @@
 }
 
 /* Do mark and sweep of unused sections.  */
+
+/* An input file that is not ELF -- an RT-11 object module among ELF
+   objects, say, which the pdp11-rt11 targets link -- has relocations of
+   its own, and the marking below cannot read them: it works through ELF
+   internal relocations.  So whatever such a file refers to is invisible
+   to the collection, and a function reached only from there would be
+   thrown away.  Worse, silently: the relocation in that file is then
+   against a symbol whose section has gone, and what it is given is
+   zero.
+
+   What a file refers to is what it leaves undefined, and that is plain
+   to read in any format.  So every symbol a non-ELF input leaves
+   undefined is kept, exactly as --undefined would keep it.  */
+
+static bool
+elf_gc_keep_non_elf_refs (struct bfd_link_info *info)
+{
+  bfd *sub;
+
+  for (sub = info->input_bfds; sub != NULL; sub = sub->link.next)
+    {
+      long symsize;
+      long symcount;
+      long i;
+      asymbol **syms;
+
+      if (bfd_get_flavour (sub) == bfd_target_elf_flavour
+	  || (sub->flags & DYNAMIC) != 0
+	  || (bfd_get_file_flags (sub) & HAS_SYMS) == 0)
+	continue;
+
+      symsize = bfd_get_symtab_upper_bound (sub);
+      if (symsize < 0)
+	return false;
+      if (symsize == 0)
+	continue;
+
+      syms = (asymbol **) bfd_malloc (symsize);
+      if (syms == NULL)
+	return false;
+
+      symcount = bfd_canonicalize_symtab (sub, syms);
+      if (symcount < 0)
+	{
+	  free (syms);
+	  return false;
+	}
+
+      for (i = 0; i < symcount; i++)
+	{
+	  struct elf_link_hash_entry *h;
+
+	  if (!bfd_is_und_section (syms[i]->section))
+	    continue;
+
+	  h = elf_link_hash_lookup (elf_hash_table (info), syms[i]->name,
+				    false, false, true);
+	  if (h != NULL
+	      && (h->root.type == bfd_link_hash_defined
+		  || h->root.type == bfd_link_hash_defweak)
+	      && !bfd_is_abs_section (h->root.u.def.section)
+	      && !bfd_is_und_section (h->root.u.def.section))
+	    h->root.u.def.section->flags |= SEC_KEEP;
+	}
+
+      free (syms);
+    }
 
+  return true;
+}
+
 bool
 bfd_elf_gc_sections (bfd *abfd, struct bfd_link_info *info)
 {
@@ -14663,6 +14747,12 @@
     }
 
   bed->gc_keep (info);
+
+  /* And what an input in another format refers to, which the marking
+     below would not see.  */
+  if (!elf_gc_keep_non_elf_refs (info))
+    return false;
+
   htab = elf_hash_table (info);
 
   /* Try to parse each bfd's .eh_frame section.  Point elf_eh_frame_section
